\date{2026-02-11}
\import{base-macros}
\taxon{VU-AL-2026}

\title{Lecture 2}

\subtree[006q]{
  \title{State in a [frame](006j)}
  \p{
    A state in a frame is just an element of the set of possible worlds #{w \in W} in the frame #{\mathcal F = \{W, R\}}
  }
  \subtree[006r]{
    \taxon{Example}
    \title{Examples of states in a frame}
    \p{
      To provide some examples of states in a frame let's look at the diagram from Lecture 1 again
    }
    \ltexfig{
      https://q.uiver.app/#q=WzAsNCxbMCwxLCIxIl0sWzEsMCwiMiJdLFsyLDEsIjQiXSxbMSwyLCIzIl0sWzAsMV0sWzEsMl0sWzAsM10sWzMsMl0sWzAsMl0sWzEsMV1d
    }{
      \[\begin{tikzcd}
        & 2 & \\
        1 && 4 \\
        & 3
        \arrow[from=1-2, to=1-2, loop, in=55, out=125, distance=10mm]
         \arrow[from=1-2, to=2-3]
         \arrow[from=2-1, to=1-2]
         \arrow[from=2-1, to=2-3]
         \arrow[from=2-1, to=3-2]
         \arrow[from=3-2, to=2-3]
      \end{tikzcd}\]
    }
    \p{
      There are various states we can assess in this frame diagram
    }
    \ul{
      \li{
        \blockquote{
          4 is the only state in which #{\Box \bot} is true
        }
        \strong{Explanation}: We know this since the local truth for the \em{necessarily} modality is defined as
        ##{
          (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \Box \phi \iff \forall v : (wRv) \to (\mathcal M, v) \vDash \phi
        }
        we know this means that #{\Box \bot} is true at a state only if it's true at all successor states. As we can trivially observe that the point #{4} has no successors or target nodes, what this means is that we have vacuous truth.
      }
      \li{
        \blockquote{
          3 is the only state in which #{\Diamond \Box \bot \land \Box \Box \bot} is true
        }
        \strong{Explanation}: We can break this down into the two conjuncts
        \ol{
          \li{
            #{\Diamond \Box \bot} - we have both modalities here, we can read this as: there exists some successor for whom all successors are true. Here again our vacuous condition kicks in, 3 indeed has some successor namely 4, and we know from the earlier example that 4 is vacuously true for #{\Box \bot}.
          }
          \li{
            #{\Box \Box \bot} - This should hopefully be quite straightforward by now as its just equivalent to the above formula given that there only is one successor to 3.
          }
        }
      }
      \li{
        \blockquote{
          1 is the only state in which #{\Diamond (\Diamond \Box \bot \land \Box \Box \bot)} is true
        }
        Given what we know from the prior formula this should be relatively straightforward, we know that entire inner section is only true in state/world 3 hence going back to our definition of local truth for #{\Diamond}
        ##{
          (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \Diamond \phi \iff \exists v: (w R v) \land (\mathcal M, v) \vDash \phi
        }
        we clearly only have #{(\mathcal M, 1) \vDash \Diamond(\phi)} where #{v = 3}.
      }
      \li{
        \blockquote{
          2 is the only state in which #{\Diamond \Diamond \top \land \neg \Diamond (\Diamond \Box \bot \land \Box \Box \bot)} is true
        }
        Probably makes sense to again break this into the two sides of the conjunct
        \ol{
          \li{
            This part seems interesting, if we use our expression again we have
            ##{
              \exists v: (w R v) \land (\exists v': (v R v') \land \top)
            }
            in plain English I'm fairly certain this is basically just asking which state/world has to successors as the final #{(\mathcal M, v') \vDash \top} is just a tautology, here we technically have 2 states for which this holds, but due to the second conjunct it filters things down to just the state 2. (I think)
          }
          \li{
            This seems less interesting as we can just infer it's an exclusion of 1 since that's the only state in which that expression is true.
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  \subtree[006s]{
    \taxon{Definition}
    \title{Local truth: some more examples}
    \p{
      To expand on some more examples of local truth we can enumerate them below
    }
    ##{
      \begin{align*}
      (\mathcal M, w) \nvDash \Box p & \iff \exists v \in W : (wRv) \land (\mathcal M, v) \nvDash p \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \nvDash \Diamond p & \iff \forall v \in W  : (wRv) \land (\mathcal M, v) \nvDash p \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \top & \iff \textrm{always} \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \bot & \iff \textrm{never} \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \Diamond \top & \iff \exists v \in W : (wRv) \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \Box \bot &\iff \ \mathrlap{\,/}{\exists}\ v \in W : (wRv) \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \nvDash \Diamond \bot &\iff \textrm{always} \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \Box \top &\iff \textrm{always} \\
      (\mathcal M, w) \vDash \Diamond \bot &\iff \textrm{never}
      \end{align*}
    }
    \p{
      The sort of premise I think when it comes to modalities prepended to #{\bot} and #{\top} I think is more or less just analogous to asking if something does or doesn't have a successor. Hence, why #{(\mathcal M, w) \nvDash \Diamond \bot} for example is always true, the line of reasoning being
      \ol{
        \li{
          The model "possibly" needs some successor (where the formula holds)
        }
        \li{
          #{\bot} definitionally holds nowhere
        }
        \li{
          Hence, it's never the case we have a successor where the formula is true as the formula is just itself #{\bot}
        }
      }
    }
  }
  \subtree[006t]{
    \taxon{Definition}
    \title{Characterizing and distinguishing states}
    \p{
      We say that a formula \em{characterizes} a state #{x} in a model #{\mathcal{M}} if #{\phi} is true in #{x} but not in the other states of #{\mathcal{M}}. We say that a formula #{\phi} \em{distinguishes} a state #{x} from a state #{y} in a model #{\mathcal{M}} if #{\phi} is true in #{x} but not in #{y}.
    }
    \subtree[006u]{
      \taxon{Example}
      \title{Modal formulas characterizing states}
      \p{
        As an example question let's say we want to find for every point #{i} a characterizing formula #{\phi_i}, that is, a formula
        ##{
          (\mathcal{M}, i) \vDash \phi_j \iff i = j.\ \forall i, j \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\}
        }
      }
      \ltexfig{
        https://q.uiver.app/#q=WzAsNCxbMCwwLCIxIl0sWzEsMCwiMiJdLFsxLDEsIjMiXSxbMCwxLCI0Il0sWzAsMV0sWzEsMl0sWzMsMl0sWzAsM11d
      }{
        \[\begin{tikzcd}
          1 & 2 \\
          4 & 3
          \arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
          \arrow[from=1-1, to=2-1]
          \arrow[from=1-2, to=2-2]
          \arrow[from=2-1, to=2-2]
        \end{tikzcd}\]
      }
    }
  }
}
